/-
# Chernikov involution centralizers in simple groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 4.36)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 4.36 (O. H. Kegel): is there an infinite
locally finite simple group `G` with an involution `i` such that the centralizer `C_G(i)` is a
Chernikov group?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.OrderOfElement
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.ChernikovSubgroupLatticeDimensionKourovka11Problem116Wanted
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.LocallyFiniteSimpleFiniteRankKourovka1Problem23Wanted

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.LocallyFiniteSimpleChernikovCentralizerKourovka4Problem36Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 4.36 (O. H.
Kegel). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700244 (`KOU-4.36`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

A Chernikov group is a finite extension of a direct product of finitely many quasicyclic (Prüfer)
groups: `IsChernikov` and `IsQuasicyclic` of the Kourovka 11.116 entry, reused here (a normal
subgroup of finite index isomorphic to `Q₁ × ⋯ × Q_r` with each `Qᵢ` infinite abelian with all
proper subgroups finite, `r = 0` allowed). An involution is an element of order `2`. Locally finite
(the subgroup generated by every finite set is finite) is `IsLocallyFinite` of the Kourovka 1.23
entry, reused here.
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ChernikovSubgroupLatticeDimensionKourovka11Problem116Wanted
open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.LocallyFiniteSimpleFiniteRankKourovka1Problem23Wanted

/-- Kourovka 4.36 (O. H. Kegel): there is an infinite locally finite simple group `G` with an
involution `i` whose centralizer `C_G(i)` is a Chernikov group. -/
def existsInfiniteLocallyFiniteSimpleChernikovInvolutionCentralizer : Prop :=
  ∃ (G : Type) (_ : Group G) (i : G), Infinite G ∧ IsSimpleGroup G ∧ IsLocallyFinite G ∧
    orderOf i = 2 ∧ IsChernikov (Subgroup.centralizer {i})

/--
Resolved false: Asar shows that no infinite locally finite simple group has an involution with a
Chernikov centralizer; the Kourovka Archive answers 4.36 "No". Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D.
Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue,
arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 4.36 (O. H. Kegel); resolution: A. O.
Asar, The solution of a problem of Kegel and Wehrfritz, Proc. London Math. Soc. (3) 45 (1982),
337-364, Kourovka Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 4.36: No,
https://doi.org/10.1112/plms/s3-45.2.337.
-/
public theorem_wanted existsInfiniteLocallyFiniteSimpleChernikovInvolutionCentralizer_refuted :
    ¬ existsInfiniteLocallyFiniteSimpleChernikovInvolutionCentralizer

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.LocallyFiniteSimpleChernikovCentralizerKourovka4Problem36Wanted
